.TH FUZZYGENE 1 2000-1月 "fuzzygene_rank_perturbations" "fuzzygene_rank_perturbations"
.SH NAME
fuzzygene_rank_perturbations \- Rank candidate perturbations by their predicted response strength.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fIfuzzygene_rank_perturbations(\fBfuzzygene\fR as <generic> FuzzyGeneModel, 
\fBcandidates\fR as integer = 24, 
\fBmode\fR as string = "knockout", 
[\fB<Environment>\fR]);\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Rank candidate perturbations by their predicted response strength.
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\fBfuzzygene\fB \fR\- a trained (or restored) model. 
.PP
.PP
\fBcandidates\fB \fR\- number of candidate genes to evaluate; 0 means all modelled genes. 
.PP
.PP
\fBmode\fB \fR\- \fBknockout\fR (default), \fBknockdown\fR or \fBoverexpression\fR. 
.PP
.PP
\fBenv\fB \fR\- the R# runtime environment used to report errors. 
.PP
.SH VALUE
.PP
a data.frame \fBgene\fR / \fBmax_abs_z\fR / \fBaffected\fR / \fBelapsed_ms\fR, best first
.PP
.SH DETAILS
.PP
Running a perturbation for every gene of a genome scale model takes hours, so only
 **candidates** genes (an evenly spaced sample of the modelled genes, no
 random numbers involved) are evaluated.
 


 


 The score is the largest `|Z|` among the genes that were not clamped: the Z score
 of the target itself is the imposed shift, not a model prediction. Note that with
 \fBclampToSupport\fR enabled the largest `|Z|` often saturates at the same value for many
 candidates; the \fBaffected\fR column (number of significant genes) is the tie breaker, and
 is a better ranking criterion in that case.
.PP
.SH SEE ALSO
FuzzyGene
